From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 12:03:45 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: lose the device when the Metal command queue
 stops

A signal this driver hands Metal is delivered by the queue it was enqueued
on, so a queue that stops running work never delivers it and never says so.
Nothing else notices: the queue reports nothing further, so the retirement
that replaces a failed queue never triggers, the failure count the signal
recovery keys on stays zero, and every waiter blocks -- in this process, in
the renderer's per-ring sync thread, and through the guest protocol in the
guest.  An iPad Vulkan CTS fraction spent 2,700 seconds in that state, paying
thirty seconds of host escape for every test case, with not one line from the
driver.

The one bound that could have caught it, kk_timeline_recover_lost_signal,
called kk_queue_complete_owed_signals, whose body runs only while a reported
failure is outstanding.  That is right for what it repairs -- a signal a
failure destroyed -- and exactly wrong here, because no failure was reported.

Split the two cases where the evidence separates them.  A queue carrying a
reported failure destroyed the signal, and the repair is unchanged.  A queue
that has reported no failure destroyed nothing, so it accepted the signal and
stopped: that is the disposition kk_queue_error.h already gives a queue that
stops reporting, and the device is lost, with a report naming the value, the
bound and how many submissions the queue has not answered for.  The bound for
losing a device is three times the one for completing a value, because it is
final and must not be reached by a queue that is merely busy.

Both bounds run on the one clock the timeline keeps while it stands still
owing a signal, so waiters on different values of a stopped queue's timeline
reach the bound together; the new stall mode of the timeline wait test holds
two such waiters and requires both to see the device lost.

A query wait had the same blind spot. vkGetQueryPoolResults with WAIT
observes a GPU write directly in memory and bounded that wait only once the
queue had failed, so a query behind a queue that stopped without failing
spun forever. It now loses the device at the same stopped-queue bound,
counted from the last submission the queue answered for, while the queue
holds work it has not answered for; a queue holding nothing still waits as
long as it takes, because the query may belong to work not yet submitted.
The bound moves to kk_queue.h for both waits, and kk_queue_get_progress,
which replaces kk_queue_has_failed, reads the queue's failure history and its
answered and unanswered submissions under one lock. The wait test's new
query mode holds a query behind a submission the queue never answers for and
requires the device lost between 30 and 45 s.
